d. tolerance and non-discrimination in differences of race, between groups, gender, religion, ethnicity, age, economic background and social status.

6.9.text:
Article 3
(1) The objectives of implementing protection for women and children victims of violence are to:
a. prevent the occurrence of all forms of violence against women and children;
b. provide initial handling of victims;
c. provide health services for victims both physical and psychological; d. provide guarantees of protection, security, and legal certainty to women and children victims of violence, witnesses, and reporters; e. pursue recovery of victims and rehabilitation;
f. empower victims and reintegration;
g. conduct coordination and evaluation of handling violence against women and children.
(2) Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of: a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic neglect; and
e. restriction of public access.

CHAPTER VI
PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 10
(1) To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall conduct empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, counseling and/or guidance.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall undertake efforts as follows: a. increase the quantity and quality of education both formal, non-formal and informal;
b. increase accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement, and social services; c. open employment opportunities for women;
d. build community participation and concern towards the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence; e. build and provide a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. build networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. open complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence based at the sub-district level down to the village.

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
The implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:
a. humanity, including respect for the rights of women and children, protection and security for victims, best service, affordable and fast, sensitivity and empathy, maintaining victim confidentiality;
b. justice, including equality, legal certainty, presumption of innocence, equal rights before the law, best interests of victims, not blaming victims;
c. transparency, accountability, and participatory;
d. tolerance and non-discrimination in differences of race, between groups, gender, religion, ethnicity, age, economic background and social status.

6.10.text:
Article 5
Child victims of acts of violence in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 4, also obtain special rights, as follows: a. the right to obtain respect and services for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to be free from negative stigma; and
d. the right to obtain freedom to determine choices in deciding the management of themselves after the divorce of parents.

CHAPTER III
RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE Article 4 Women and children victims of acts of violence shall receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected; b. the right to prompt, and affordable complaint handling and services. c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process;
h. the right to assistance;
i. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim; and
j. the right to rehabilitation and social reintegration.

